iles.\n\nMaybe I should consider the possibility of \"Chicken Hand\" which is 0 fans but with no flowers? But Chicken Hand is worth 8 fans? Actually Chicken Hand is a pattern worth 8 fans, defined as a hand that would otherwise be worth 0 fans. But my hand currently has 4 fans from winds, so not 0. So Chicken Hand not applicable.\n\nGiven the situation, I think the best is to discard the tile that is less likely to give opponents a win or a useful meld. Between B3 and F4: B3 is a simple tile, and across player already has B3 pung, so maybe he is not waiting for B3. F4 is a wind tile, and wind pungs are common for fans. But no one has shown F4 meld. However, previous player discarded F4 once, so he might not need it. I'll check histories: Previous player played F4 once (in his history). Next player didn't play F4. Across didn't play F4. So F4 might be relatively safe.\n\nAlso, from unshown tiles, there are 2 F4 left and 1 B3 left. So B3 is rarer. If I discard B3, there will be 0 B3 left unshown? Actually unshown B3:1, if I discard my B3, then that B3 becomes shown, so unshown becomes 0. So then no B3 left to draw for me to pair. But if I discard B3, I keep F4, then I need F4 to win. There are 2 F4 left, so better chance. So keeping F4 gives me 2 outs, keeping B3 gives 1 out. So for winning probability, keeping F4 is better. But winning now is insufficient fans, so maybe I don't want to win yet. But if I don't win, I might eventually draw a tile that allows me to improve fans? For example, if I draw a dragon, I could try to get a dragon pair for extra fan? But with only one tile in hand after discard, I'd need to pair it or draw a tile that can combine with it to form a chow? But I already have 4 melds, so any new meld would require breaking an existing meld, which is not allowed because melds are fixed once declared. So the only way to change hand composition is to form a kong, which doesn't change the meld count. So essentially, my hand is locked into these 4 melds.